<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>com.tao</groupId>
    <artifactId>tao-sandbox</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>tao-sandbox-ai-experimental</artifactId>
  <name>tao-sandbox :: ai-experimental</name>
  <description>
    Experimental AI capabilities for the sandbox. First intended use: generate mock payloads from a
    schema and a prompt. Deliberately separate so experiments cannot destabilise the mock service.
  </description>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Generation needs the parsed contracts and the validator, both of which are stable public API
      of the mock library: SpecRegistry supplies the schema a payload must satisfy, and
      MockValidator is what decides whether the generated one does. Depending on the library is
      the plain answer — see the note in ai/package-info.java, which posed this and now records it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tao</groupId>
      <artifactId>tao-sandbox-mock-data</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      The provider-neutral chat API — ChatModel, Prompt, the message types — and the OpenAI
      implementation of it. Azure is reached through the latter: as of Spring AI 2.0, the dedicated
      Azure OpenAI chat module is gone and its own reference page redirects here, because the wire
      format was always OpenAI's and Azure differs only in where it lives and how you prove who you
      are.

      The library, deliberately, and not `spring-ai-starter-model-openai`. The starter's
      auto-configuration builds a ChatModel from its own properties as soon as it is on the
      classpath, which would undo the one rule this module is built around: with no endpoint
      configured there is no client, no generator, and nothing that can quietly answer.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.ai</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-ai-openai</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Identity only — not an Azure OpenAI SDK. The wire format is OpenAI's and is spoken over the
      HTTP client this application already has; what Azure genuinely adds is how a caller proves
      who it is, and that is worth a library rather than a hand-rolled OAuth exchange. It is also
      what makes a service principal locally and a managed identity in Azure the same code path.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.azure</groupId>
      <artifactId>azure-identity</artifactId>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>
